id: 17
hanzi: 银子
pinyin: yín zi
level: HSK 5
part_of_speech: Noun

meanings_summary:
  en: silver; money; silver currency
  es: plata; dinero de plata; moneda

senses[1]{sense_number,part_of_speech,register,definition_en,definition_es,example_phrase}:
  1 | Noun | Standard | silver metal used historically as currency, ingots, or money | plata; dinero o moneda de plata en la antigüedad | 几两银子 (a few taels of silver)

example_sentences[2]{id,hanzi,pinyin,en,es}:
  #1 | 拿到主人发放的几两沉甸甸的银子作为工钱，他舍不得多花一分，小心翼翼地缝进内衣口袋里。 | Nádào zhǔrén fāfàng de jǐ liǎng chéndiāndiān de yínzi zuòwéi gōngqián, tā shěbude duō huā yì fēn, xiǎoxīn-yìyì de féng jìn nèiyī kǒudài lǐ. | Receiving several heavy taels of silver from the master as wages, he was loath to spend even a penny, carefully sewing them into his inner garment pocket. | Al recibir del patrón unos pesados taeles de plata como salario, no se atrevió a gastar ni un céntimo y los cosió con cuidado en el bolsillo de su ropa interior.
  #2 | 在中国古代货币史中，白银与铜钱长期并行流通，银子通常用于大宗商品贸易与官府赋税结算。 | Zài Zhōngguó gǔdài huòbì shǐ zhōng, báiyín yǔ tóngqián chángqī bìngxíng liútōng, yínzi tōngcháng yòngyú dàzōng shāngpǐn màoyì yǔ guānfǔ fùshuì jiésuàn. | In ancient Chinese monetary history, silver and copper coins circulated in tandem, with silver ingots commonly used for bulk commerce and official tax settlement. | En la historia monetaria de la antigua China, la plata y las monedas de cobre coexistieron, empleándose la plata para el gran comercio y el pago de tributos estatales.

collocations[6]{hanzi,en}:
  几两银子 | several taels of silver
  白花花的银子 | gleaming white silver
  积攒银子 | save up silver / money
  碎银子 | broken bits of silver
  赏赐银子 | bestow silver as a reward
  兑换银子 | exchange into silver

idioms[4]{hanzi,pinyin,meaning_en}:
  金银财宝 | jīn yín cái bǎo | gold, silver, and treasures
  银装素裹 | yín zhuāng sù guǒ | clad in silvery white (snow-covered landscape)
  火树银花 | huǒ shù yín huā | fiery trees and silver flowers (festive fireworks/lanterns)
  真金不怕火炼 | zhēn jīn bú pà huǒ liàn | true gold fears no fire; sterling character stands any test

character_decomposition[2]{character,pinyin,meaning_en,meaning_es,radical,radical_meaning_en,meaning_component,sound_component,stroke_count,composition_type,etymology_and_mnemonic,visual_mnemonic_story_en,visual_mnemonic_story_es,tone_mnemonic_anchor,cangjie,traditional}:
  银 | yín | silver; money; lustrous white | plata; dinero plateado | 钅 | metal / gold | 钅/金 (precious metal / ore) | 艮 (gèn / boundary/firm phonetic) | 11 | 左右结构 | Phono-semantic compound: 钅 (metal) + 艮 (firm/luster), denoting the lustrous, gleaming white precious metal. | A gleaming metal (钅) forged into firm, enduring ingots (艮). | Un metal brillante (钅) fundido en sólidos lingotes de gran pureza (艮). | 35 | CAVE | 銀
  子 | zi | noun suffix; diminutive suffix | sufijo de sustantivo | 子 | child / seed | 子 (wrapped baby) | "" | 3 | 单一结构 | Ancient pictograph of a child, grammaticalized as a ubiquitous colloquial noun suffix. | A child marker attached to concrete nouns to make them accessible and spoken. | Un elemento derivativo que confiere a los nombres cotidianos una textura cercana y familiar. | 0 | ND | ""

mnemonics_and_roots:
  composite_memory_hook_en: Precious gleaming metal (银) formed into everyday spending currency (子) is silver money (银子).
  composite_memory_hook_es: El metal brillante (银) moldeado como divisa de cambio en la vida diaria (子) es la plata amonedada (银子).
  cultural_and_cognitive_roots: In imperial Chinese society, silver ingots symbolized purchasing power and security. Ordinary laborers worked tirelessly for months just to earn a few taels of silver to support families.
  tone_sandhi_and_pitch_modulation: Rising 2nd tone followed by neutral tone: yín zi (35-0). 'zi' is pronounced lightly with reduced vowel length.

traps_and_cognates:
